//! LONG OPERATIONS, MADE INTERRUPTIBLE (design 1, M3 unit 1.5).
//!
//! Two movers, and they are `mem`'s two byte loops with a CADENCE around them:
//! every `PREEMPT_STRIDE` bytes the operation stops and asks the machine whether
//! it wants anything (`irq.preempt_point`). Everything that copies or clears in
//! bulk goes through these — the loader's segment placement today, unit 2's
//! CreateProcess image copy next — so the audit sentence for a long copy is
//! written ONCE, here, instead of once per caller.
//!
//! # WHY THE POINTS ARE NOT IN `mem` BESIDE THE LOOPS THEY DRIVE
//!
//! Design 1 asked for the points INSIDE `copy_bytes`/`zero_bytes`, with no twin
//! and no parameter. They cannot go there, and the reason is a genuine cycle
//! rather than a tidiness question — it is recorded in the design's as-built
//! section and restated here because this module IS the resolution.
//!
//! A point delivers, so it needs `irq.deliver_line`. Delivery reaches down:
//! `deliver_line` -> `on_timer_interrupt` -> `expire_timers` -> `fire_timer` ->
//! `wake.notify_ready` -> `wake_one_waiter` -> `deliver_attachment` ->
//! `process.copy_out` -> `mem.copy_bytes`. So the byte loop is BELOW delivery in
//! the module order and delivery is ABOVE it, and a point written in the loop
//! would be `mem` importing `irq` — the cycle DF-232e diagnoses. There is no
//! indirection to route around it either: a `FuncPointer` hook installed at boot
//! would need a mutable static holding a function address, and a static
//! initializer is a constant expression (`static X must be initialized by a
//! compile-time constant`), so the hook cannot even be declared.
//!
//! WHAT SURVIVES OF THE ORIGINAL SHAPE, which is what matters: the byte loop
//! exists ONCE (in `mem`), the point exists ONCE (in `irq`), and the cadence that
//! joins them exists ONCE (here). Nothing is duplicated and no call site chooses
//! a policy — it chooses an ALTITUDE, and the two altitudes are named for what
//! they are. `mem`'s loops are the primitive; these are the operation.
//!
//! WHAT DOES NOT SURVIVE: a bulk copy issued from BELOW this module cannot take
//! a point. Today that is only `process.copy_out`/`copy_in`, whose one bulk
//! caller is a wait record of 24 bytes — under a single stride, and inert under
//! `IN_DELIVERY` anyway. §2.1's Pipe bodies are the length that will want one,
//! and their callers (`dispatch`) sit above this module, so the door can grow a
//! pointed sibling THERE when it does. `mem`'s movers say so at their site.

import kcore.mem.{copy_bytes, zero_bytes}
import kcore.irq.{preempt_point}

/// How many bytes a long operation moves between preemption points.
///
/// A POINT COSTS A CONTROLLER ROUND TRIP — a claim read on one profile, an
/// acknowledge register on the other — so polling per byte would multiply a
/// 240 KiB copy by that cost. 4 KiB is design 1's D-3 starting value, and the
/// reasoning is a pair of bounds: a stride's worth of byte moves is tens of
/// microseconds of masked latency, which is the number this unit exists to bring
/// down, and one
/// poll per 4096 moves is under 1% of the copy, which is what it may cost to do
/// it. Both bounds are loose; the number is meant to be re-measured, not
/// defended.
///
/// It is a page on both profiles, which is not why it was chosen but is why it
/// reads well beside a loader that places page-aligned segments.
public(package) static PREEMPT_STRIDE: UInt = 4096

/// Copy `len` bytes from `src` to `dst`, stopping to serve the machine every
/// `PREEMPT_STRIDE` bytes.
///
/// THE POINT IS AFTER EACH CHUNK, and design 1's audit sentence for it is:
/// partially-written destination bytes are no IRQ-read state — nothing on the
/// interrupt path reads the region being filled — so between two chunks there is
/// no in-flight invariant for a delivery to land inside. The caller still owes
/// the sentence for ITS OWN state: a caller holding a half-built structure the
/// IRQ path can see must finish it before it copies.
public func long_copy(dst: UInt, src: UInt, len: UInt) unsafe {
    var done: UInt = 0
    while done < len {
        var chunk = len - done
        if chunk > PREEMPT_STRIDE {
            chunk = PREEMPT_STRIDE
        }
        copy_bytes(dst + done, src + done, chunk)
        done = done + chunk
        preempt_point()
    }
}

/// Clear `len` bytes at `dst`, stopping to serve the machine every
/// `PREEMPT_STRIDE` bytes.
///
/// Same sentence as `long_copy`'s, and this is the one that matters most today:
/// a root image's `.bss` is the largest single masked window the kernel has.
public func long_zero(dst: UInt, len: UInt) unsafe {
    var done: UInt = 0
    while done < len {
        var chunk = len - done
        if chunk > PREEMPT_STRIDE {
            chunk = PREEMPT_STRIDE
        }
        zero_bytes(dst + done, chunk)
        done = done + chunk
        preempt_point()
    }
}
